-- title:
--   q-divisible norm-free systems reducing into the torus vanish
-- statement:
--   Arithmetic data. Let $p$ be a prime, let $M$ be a natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ for the set of primes $\{p\}$, and let $\zeta \in L$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \operatorname{LaurentSeries} L$, assumed (hypothesis `hK`) to equal [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ realised inside $\mathbb{Q}((q))$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with an $L$-algebra structure making $L$ its fraction field, such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ in $L$, and let $K$ be an $A$-algebra compatibly with $A \to L \to K$. Let $j \in K$ be nonzero with image in $\operatorname{LaurentSeries} L$ equal to the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), the $q$-expansion $q^{-1} \cdot \mathrm{jNumQ}$ of the $j$-invariant. Write $X =$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) for the resulting two-chart model over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, which is assumed proper.
--
--   Special fibre data. Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ which is an $A$-algebra, and let $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$, geometrically integral. Let $i_1, i_2$ be closed immersions of $C_1$, $C_2$ into the base change of $X$ along $A \to k$, over $\operatorname{Spec} k$. The hypothesis `hcover` requires every point of that base change to lie in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` requires the fibre product of $i_1$ and $i_2$ to be reduced, and `hn`, `hn0` require its number of points to be $n$ with $0 < n$.
--
--   Sections. Let $\varepsilon$ be a section of $X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ and $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ sections of $c_1, c_2$, with `hε₁` requiring $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ to be the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$.
--
--   Relative Picard data. Let $D$ consist of a scheme $D.P$ with a structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section; `hrep` asserts that $D$ represents, via a Poincaré rigidified line bundle, the functor of rigidified line bundles on $X$ along $\varepsilon$ that are fibrewise algebraically equivalent to zero (the cut `algEquivZeroCut`), and $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is assumed smooth (`hsm`) and separated (`hsep`). The hypothesis `hreps` asserts the corresponding representability over $k$ for the base change of $X$ and of $D$, and `hPk` requires the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` to be isomorphic to the base change, in the sense of `BaseChange.ofR`, of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep` along the first projection of $D.P \times_{\operatorname{Spec} A} \operatorname{Spec} k$. Likewise $D_1$, $D_2$ with `hrep₁`, `hrep₂` represent the corresponding functors for $c_1$ and $c_2$ with the sections $\varepsilon_1$, $\varepsilon_2$. Let $\nu_2$ be a morphism from the base change of $D$ to $k$ into $D_2$, over $\operatorname{Spec} k$; the hypothesis `hν₂` requires that for every $k$-scheme $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and every $T$-point $a$ of the base-changed $D$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep₂` along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification along `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` of the pullback along `curveChange i₂.1 i₂.2 t` of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` along $a$; thus $\nu_2$ computes restriction of line bundles to $C_2$ together with rigidification at $\varepsilon_2$.
--
--   Embeddings into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. The algebraic closure $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ is an $A$-algebra and an $L$-algebra, compatibly.
--
--   Dictionary for the special fibre. Let $G$ be a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9), that is: abelian groups $J^0_s$, $J_I$, $J_E$, a subgroup $T \le J^0_s$ and a surjective homomorphism $\mathrm{proj} : J^0_s \to J_I \times J_E$ with kernel $T$. Let $\mathrm{pts}$, $\mathrm{ptsI}$, $\mathrm{ptsE}$ be bijections from $J^0_s$, $J_I$, $J_E$ onto the $k$-points of the base change of $D$, of $D_1$ and of $D_2$ respectively. The hypotheses `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` require these bijections to be additive at the level of Poincaré pullbacks: the pullback along the point attached to $a + b$ is isomorphic to the tensor product of the pullbacks along the points attached to $a$ and to $b$. The hypothesis `hproj` requires, for each $x \in J^0_s$, that $\mathrm{ptsI}$ of the first component of $\mathrm{proj}(x)$ be $\mathrm{pts}(x)$ followed by the classifying morphism `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁.1 i₁.2 hε₁ hreps hrep₁.some`, and that $\mathrm{ptsE}$ of the second component be $\mathrm{pts}(x)$ followed by $\nu_2$.
--
--   Generic dictionary and Hecke maps. Let $\mathrm{gpts}$ be a bijection from $J_1(Mp) = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the function field `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ onto the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, additive for the relative group law attached to `hrep.some` (hypothesis `hgadd`). Let $\varphi$ assign to each element of the Hecke algebra [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) $= \mathrm{MvPolynomial}\,(\mathrm{Primes} \sqcup \mathbb{N})\,\mathbb{Z}$ a self-morphism of $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$.
--
--   The subscheme $\mathcal{A}$. Let $a : \mathcal{A} \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ and let $\iota$ be a morphism from $\mathcal{A}$ to $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ which is a closed immersion (`h𝒜cl`), with $a$ proper (`h𝒜pr`), smooth (`h𝒜sm`) and with all geometric fibres connected (`h𝒜conn`: for every algebraically closed field and every morphism of its spectrum to $\operatorname{Spec} A$, the fibre product is a connected space). The hypothesis `h𝒜grp` requires that for every $s : T \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ the points of $D$ of the form $x$ followed by $\iota$, for $x$ a $T$-point of $\mathcal{A}$, contain the unit of the relative group law of `hrep.some` and be closed under its multiplication and inversion. The hypothesis `h𝒜gen` requires that an element $x$ of $J_1(Mp)$ lie in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) — the range of the endomorphism `normFreeEnd (M * p) (normFreeRepsAt (M * p) p)` of $J_1(Mp)$ — if and only if the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\mathrm{gpts}(x)$ factors through $\iota$. The hypothesis `h𝒜hecke` requires, for every Hecke element $t$, every $s : T \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ and every $T$-point $x$ of $\mathcal{A}$, that $x$ followed by $\iota$ and then by $\varphi(t)$ again factor through $\iota$.
--
--   Valuation data. Let $\mathrm{Pl}$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit of $\mathrm{Pl}$ (the predicate [`ValuationSubring.LiesOverPrime`](def/FLTPrelim_Ramification.html#L16)), let $\rho : A \to \mathrm{Pl}$ be a ring homomorphism inducing $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, let $O$ be a subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ contained in $\mathrm{Pl}$ with a ring homomorphism $\rho_O : A \to O$ inducing $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and let $\pi_k : \mathrm{Pl} \to k$ be a ring homomorphism with $A \to k$ equal to $\pi_k \circ \rho$.
--
--   Conclusion. For every natural number $q$, every sequence $x : \mathbb{N} \to J_1(Mp)$, every sequence $z$ of $O$-points of $D$ (points of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ over the morphism $\operatorname{Spec} O \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ induced by $\rho_O$) and every sequence $y : \mathbb{N} \to J^0_s$ such that
--
--   (i) $q \cdot x_0 = 0$;
--
--   (ii) $q \cdot x_{n+1} = x_n$ for all $n$;
--
--   (iii) $x_n \in$ [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) for all $n$;
--
--   (iv) for all $n$, the underlying morphism of $\mathrm{gpts}(x_n)$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the inclusion $O \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ followed by the underlying morphism of $z_n$;
--
--   (v) for all $n$, the underlying morphism of $\mathrm{pts}(y_n)$ followed by the first projection from the base change of $D$ to $k$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\pi_k \circ (O \hookrightarrow \mathrm{Pl})$ followed by the underlying morphism of $z_n$;
--
--   (vi) $\mathrm{proj}(y_n) = 0$, i.e. $y_n$ lies in the subgroup $T$, for all $n$;
--
--   one has $y_n = 0$ for every $n$.
--
--   This is the statement that a $q$-divisible system of $q$-power-torsion classes in the norm-free part of $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ whose reductions at $p$ land in the torus part $T = \ker(\mathrm{proj})$ of the Néron special fibre of the two-chart (stable) model of $X_1(Mp)$ must reduce to zero; it reflects the fact that a proper smooth connected subgroup scheme of the Picard scheme meets the torus in a finite group. It is used in the analysis of the norm-free family attached to $J_1(Mp)$ at $p$, in the results [`ModularCurve.XOneP.normFreePartFamily_exists_addEquiv_toPic0Pair_sp_heckeOperatorOneBar_snd_eq_twoChartModel_x1_mul`](thm.html#ModularCurve.XOneP.normFreePartFamily_exists_addEquiv_toPic0Pair_sp_heckeOperatorOneBar_snd_eq_twoChartModel_x1_mul), [`ModularCurve.XOneP.normFreePartFamily_forall_apply_eq_zero_of_tateModule_toPic0Pair_sp_eq_zero_twoChartModel_x1_mul`](thm.html#ModularCurve.XOneP.normFreePartFamily_forall_apply_eq_zero_of_tateModule_toPic0Pair_sp_eq_zero_twoChartModel_x1_mul) and [`ModularCurve.XOneP.normFreePartFamily_pairing_eq_one_of_toPic0Pair_sp_eq_zero_twoChartModel_x1_mul`](thm.html#ModularCurve.XOneP.normFreePartFamily_pairing_eq_one_of_toPic0Pair_sp_eq_zero_twoChartModel_x1_mul).
